/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A number stays useless until everyone agrees on the size of the unit.
   - Since 20 May 2019 each base unit is fixed by giving an exact value to a constant of nature, so any well-equipped laboratory can realise it:
   - Every other unit on a datasheet is built from these. 1 N = 1 kg·m/s²; 1 Pa = 1 N/m²; 1 V = 1 kg·m²/(s³·A); 1 Ω = 1 V/A; 1 F = 1 C/V; 1 Hz = 1/s.
   - Two habits catch most errors.
   - You cannot check a volt against nature on a shop floor.
   - Each rung is more accurate than the one below, typically by a factor of four to ten; a standard only as good as the device under test proves nothing.
   - Traceability is what makes the ladder usable: an unbroken chain of calibrations, each link with a stated uncertainty, each documented, ending at a primary standard.
   - Calibration compares the instrument output with a known input produced by a standard, and records the result.
   - In this book %FS means a percentage of the full-scale output span: output at the upper limit minus output at the lower limit (3.98 V in the example).
   - Answer.
*/
