/* book Worked example: Q: A 0–250 kPa pressure sensor has output 0–50 mV. Ten repeated readings at a true pressure of 100 kPa have mean 99.6 kPa and sample standard deviation 0.5 kPa. The display resolution is 0.1 kPa. A calibration curve deviates from its straight line by at most 0.6 mV. Find sensitivity, bias error in kPa and %FS, precision band at 3σ in kPa and %FS, resolution in %FS, and linearity in kPa and %FS. Comment on accuracy, precision and linearity.
   1. Input span = 250 kPa; output span = 50 mV. S = 50 mV / 250 kPa = 0.2 mV/kPa.
   2. Bias = mean − true = 99.6 − 100 = −0.4 kPa. As %FS: −0.4 / 250 × 100 = −0.16 %FS.
   3. Precision band = ±3σ = ±3 × 0.5 = ±1.5 kPa. As %FS: 1.5 / 250 × 100 = ±0.6 %FS.
   4. Resolution = 0.1 kPa. As %FS: 0.1 / 250 × 100 = 0.04 %FS.
   5. Linearity in input = 0.6 mV / 0.2 mV/kPa = 3 kPa. As %FS: 3 / 250 × 100 = 1.2 %FS.
   6. Reading: the mean is close to true (bias −0.16 %FS), the 3s band of ±0.6 %FS is moderate, and linearity at 1.2 %FS is the largest error, thirty times the 0.04 %FS resolution.
   Answer: S = 0.2 mV/kPa; bias = −0.4 kPa = −0.16 %FS; precision band = ±1.5 kPa = ±0.6 %FS; resolution = 0.1 kPa = 0.04 %FS; linearity = 3 kPa = 1.2 %FS. Linearity dominates the static error; the bias is small. */
